  "notes": "The most structurally unorthodox pop of the era: 116–146 straight, backbeat on 2&4, with the research noting that the 164 reading on the debut is a doubled value over an ~82 half-time surf-guitar spine — that fold is why the session floor is 116 rather than in the 160s. The drum block is the entry's \"electro-punk programmed kit\": a driving 8th kick (density 3–6, low syncopation), a gated snare with a high accent tier, \"16th hat with heavy gating\", and \"a live-sounding tom pattern\" plus \"breakbeat/drum'n'bass inserts as transition devices\" — the tom lanes and a high `switchUpProb` are those last two. Harmony is deliberately unstable: songs change centre between sections and choruses do not resolve to the tonic, so the families mix a minor cycle, a major cycle and a non-resolving pair, and no family cadences on V. The Xenomania compositional method — \"separately-written hook cells bolted into one song, so a track can have 4–6 distinct melodic ideas and no repeated pre-chorus\" — is why the melody carries 2-, 4- and 8-bar phrases at equal weight and the arrangement uses 6-, 10- and 12-bar sections instead of 8s and 16s. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says per-song claims are catalogue-derived. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and `bpm.mode` 130.",
